# Ocentra Parent Portal Rules

The portal is a dev visibility and HMR surface. Product parent UI runs through
packaged Tauri desktop plus Android/iOS shells, and talks to Rust through
HostBridge.

Rust owns product runtime behavior, contracts, actions, route snapshots, read
models, and policy decisions. Portal TypeScript is presentation only: TSX, CSS,
assets, generated bridge DTO/type imports, thin HostBridge/DevWebHostBridge
adapters, and minimal local visual state.

WebSocket may remain a dev transport or Rust runtime/LAN concern. Do not make
WebSocket the product `TSX UI <-> parent Rust` path.

## Scope

Allowed now:

- route shells
- command buttons
- health/status panels
- HostBridge or dev-transport connection state
- real local agent events
- diagnostics that help develop the Rust parent/runtime path

Not allowed:

- web-first product architecture
- marketing pages
- fake dashboards
- fake child activity
- product promises that the service cannot prove
- TS-owned product contracts, route snapshots, policy logic, or fallback
  behavior

## UI Data Rules

- Do not render made-up device activity.
- Do not invent parent/child records.
- Text may come from pure presentation helpers only when it does not encode
  product state or policy.
- Routes, DOM ids, and command descriptors may come from transitional TS helper
  packages only when they are presentation-only. Product route snapshots and
  action/result shapes come from Rust-owned generated bridge DTOs.
- The portal calls HostBridge, DevWebHostBridge, or the real local dev service
  and clearly shows disconnected/unavailable state.
- The portal uses fixed port `4478`; LAN mode binds that port to the network through `npm run dev:lan`.
- Do not use `5173` or the Ocentra Games asset-editor port `5174`.
- When pointed at a LAN WebSocket URL in dev mode, commands should use the
  `local-network` target route. LAN product runtime ownership remains Rust-side
  and out of this portal rule.

## Interaction Rules

- Buttons should dispatch real Rust-owned/generated actions or explicit dev
  transport commands.
- Status panels should display real Rust-owned snapshots/events.
- Error states should show typed failure information, not generic success.
- Browser smoke tests should use the real portal and service.
